$DIR/main.p:15:9: cannot return &b.n, which points into the object b refers to, which b keeps alive in g
    &b.n points into the object b refers to, which b keeps alive in g. b
    (declared at main.p:13) is an owned[H]: the object it refers to lives only
    while b holds it, and is dropped when b is dropped at the end of its scope
    in g, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the object,
    or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive b. When g
    returns, b is dropped and the object freed, so &b.n would point at freed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return b itself: an owned box moves to the caller, and a shared or synced
    one gives the caller its own reference:
        return b
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
